<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter5_designing-object-interface">
  <title>Designing an Object's Interface and Behavior</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A class is more than a collection of private data members. Its public
      member functions describe how other code is allowed to interact with its
      objects.
    </p>

    <p>
      The public part of a class forms its <term>interface</term>. The
      operations provided by that interface describe the object's
      <term>behavior</term>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Good class design therefore asks two related questions:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>What should code outside the class be allowed to do?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Which operations naturally belong to the object itself?</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>The Public Interface</title>

    <p>
      Consider a simple <c>Thermostat</c> class:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Thermostat {
public:
    Thermostat(double initial_temperature);

    double get_temperature() const;
    bool set_temperature(double new_temperature);

private:
    double temperature;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Code outside the class can see and use the public constructor and member
      functions. It cannot directly access <c>temperature</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      This means the public interface communicates the intended ways to use a
      <c>Thermostat</c>:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Thermostat room{21.0};

double current = room.get_temperature();
bool changed = room.set_temperature(23.0);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The private representation is an implementation detail. Code using the
      class should depend on the public interface rather than on how the object
      stores its state.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Observer Functions</title>

    <p>
      A member function that reports information about an object without
      changing it is often called an <term>observer</term>.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double Thermostat::get_temperature() const {
    return temperature;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Because this function only observes the object, it is declared
      <c>const</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      A function whose main purpose is to return one particular data value is
      often called a <term>getter</term> or <term>accessor</term>. The name
      <c>get_temperature</c> makes that purpose clear.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, private data does not mean that every data member automatically
      needs a getter. A public operation should exist because outside code has
      a meaningful reason to use it.
    </p>

    <p>
      Sometimes an observer reports information derived from the object's
      state instead of returning a stored value directly:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
public:
    double calculate_area() const;

private:
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Here, <c>calculate_area()</c> is part of the useful interface even
      though there is no data member named <c>area</c>.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>State-Changing Functions</title>

    <p>
      Other member functions intentionally change an object's state. These
      functions do not have a trailing <c>const</c>.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
bool Thermostat::set_temperature(double new_temperature) {
    if (new_temperature < 10.0 || new_temperature > 30.0) {
        return false;
    }

    temperature = new_temperature;
    return true;
}
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      In this example, our <c>Thermostat</c> model accepts settings from
      10 through 30 degrees. The member function checks the requested value
      before changing the private data.
    </p>

    <p>
      The return value tells the caller whether the requested change
      succeeded:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
bool changed = room.set_temperature(35.0);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Since <c>35.0</c> is outside the range chosen for this model,
      <c>changed</c> is <c>false</c> and the object's previous temperature is
      preserved.
    </p>

    <p>
      A state-changing member function may be called a <term>mutator</term>.
      As with getters, the important design question is not whether a setter
      can be written, but whether the operation belongs in the object's public
      interface.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Prefer Meaningful Behavior When Possible</title>

    <p>
      A public interface often becomes clearer when its member functions
      describe meaningful actions rather than simply exposing every piece of
      stored data.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, suppose a <c>BankAccount</c> stores a balance. A design
      could provide:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
bool set_balance(double new_balance);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      But an interface based on the actions that accounts actually perform may
      communicate the design better:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
void deposit(double amount);
bool withdraw(double amount);
double get_balance() const;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The second design describes behavior: money can be deposited or
      withdrawn, and the balance can be observed. Outside code does not need
      permission to replace the balance with an arbitrary value.
    </p>

    <p>
      This does not mean that setter functions are always wrong. A setter is
      useful when directly replacing a property is itself a meaningful
      operation. The goal is to make the interface express the intended use of
      the object.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Keep the Interface Focused</title>

    <p>
      Making a member function public means that other code is invited to
      depend on it. A good interface therefore provides the operations that
      users of the object need without exposing unnecessary details.
    </p>

    <p>
      Compare these two designs:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
public:
    double get_length() const;
    double get_width() const;
    void set_length(double new_length);
    void set_width(double new_width);
    double calculate_area() const;

private:
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      and:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
class Rectangle {
public:
    bool set_dimensions(double new_length, double new_width);
    double calculate_area() const;

private:
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Neither interface is automatically correct for every program. If outside
      code genuinely needs to inspect or replace each dimension separately,
      the first design may be appropriate. If dimensions should normally be
      changed together, the second interface may better express that design.
    </p>

    <p>
      The important idea is that public functions should be chosen
      deliberately rather than generated mechanically for every private data
      member.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Member Function or Free Function?</title>

    <p>
      Not every useful operation involving an object needs to be a member
      function. Free functions remain an important part of C++.
    </p>

    <p>
      A useful question is:
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Does this operation naturally belong to this object?
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      An operation is often a good member function when it:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>observes the state of one object,</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>changes the state of one object, or</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>represents behavior that is naturally associated with that type.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      For example:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room.calculate_area();
account.deposit(50.0);
thermostat.set_temperature(22.0);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      A free function can be a better choice when the operation is more
      general and does not naturally belong to one particular object.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double calculate_distance(const Point& first, const Point& second);
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The distance describes a relationship between two points. It does not
      need to be treated as a behavior owned by either point.
    </p>

    <p>
      These are design guidelines rather than rigid rules. As programs become
      larger, choosing clear responsibilities becomes an important part of
      program design.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>A Complete Example</title>

    <p>
      The following program combines a constructor, an observer, and a
      state-changing member function in one small interface.
    </p>

    <listing label="chapter5_designing_interface_AC_1">
      <title>
        A <c>Thermostat</c> controls access to its temperature setting.
      </title>

      <program interactive="activecode"
               line-numbers="yes"
               label="chapter5_designing_interface_AC_1_editor">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Thermostat {
public:
    Thermostat(double initial_temperature);

    double get_temperature() const;
    bool set_temperature(double new_temperature);

private:
    double temperature;
};

int main() {
    Thermostat room{21.0};

    std::cout << "Initial: "
              << room.get_temperature() << '\n';

    if (room.set_temperature(23.5)) {
        std::cout << "Updated: "
                  << room.get_temperature() << '\n';
    }

    if (!room.set_temperature(35.0)) {
        std::cout << "Invalid setting rejected.\n";
    }

    std::cout << "Final: "
              << room.get_temperature() << '\n';

    return 0;
}

Thermostat::Thermostat(double initial_temperature)
    : temperature(20.0) {
    if (initial_temperature >= 10.0 &&
        initial_temperature <= 30.0) {
        temperature = initial_temperature;
    }
}

double Thermostat::get_temperature() const {
    return temperature;
}

bool Thermostat::set_temperature(double new_temperature) {
    if (new_temperature < 10.0 || new_temperature > 30.0) {
        return false;
    }

    temperature = new_temperature;
    return true;
}
        ]]></code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      The class controls its own state. Outside code can observe the
      temperature and request a change, but it cannot directly assign to the
      private data member.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise label="chapter5_designing_interface_1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which member function should normally be declared <c>const</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>double get_temperature() const;</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This function observes the object without changing its state.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>bool set_temperature(double new_temperature);</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This function may change the object's state, so it should not be
              a <c>const</c> member function.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>void deposit(double amount);</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A deposit changes the account's state.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_designing_interface_2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which interface best expresses the behavior of a bank account?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Only <c>set_balance(double new_balance)</c>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Replacing the balance directly does not express the usual
              actions performed on an account.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <c>deposit</c>, <c>withdraw</c>, and
              <c>get_balance</c>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              These operations describe meaningful account behavior while
              keeping direct control of the stored balance inside the class.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Make <c>balance</c> public and provide no member functions.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              That gives outside code unrestricted access instead of defining a
              controlled interface.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_designing_interface_3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which operation is a strong candidate for a free function rather
          than a member function?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Depositing money into one <c>BankAccount</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A deposit changes the state of one account and naturally belongs
              to that object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p>Reading one <c>Thermostat</c>'s temperature</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Observing the state of one thermostat naturally fits its public
              interface.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>Calculating the distance between two <c>Point</c> objects</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Distance describes a relationship between two points and does
              not have to belong to either point.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_designing_interface_4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why should a class not automatically provide a getter and setter for
          every private data member?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Because C++ does not allow getters and setters for private data.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Public member functions can read or modify private data members.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The public interface should expose operations that are meaningful
              for using the object, rather than exposing internal state
              mechanically.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A focused interface communicates how the object is intended to be
              used.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Because every private data member must remain permanently
              unchanged.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Private members can be changed by the class's own
              state-changing member functions.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_designing_interface_5"
              number="yes"
              adaptive="yes"
              indentation="hide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Arrange the declarations to create a focused public interface for a
          <c>Counter</c>. Outside code should be able to increase the count,
          reset it, and observe the current count, but should not directly
          modify the stored value.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <blocks>
        <block order="1">
          <cline>class Counter {</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="2">
          <cline>public:</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="3">
          <cline>    void increment();</cline>
          <cline>    void reset();</cline>
          <cline>    int get_count() const;</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="4">
          <cline>private:</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="5">
          <cline>    int count = 0;</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="6">
          <cline>};</cline>
        </block>

        <block correct="no">
          <cline>public: int count = 0;</cline>
        </block>

        <block correct="no">
          <cline>    int get_count();</cline>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

</section>
